medium|[medium] Use checked arithmetic in contracts/mock_token|`contracts/mock_token/src/lib.rs`|`mint`, `move_balance` and the recipient credit use plain `+` and `-` on `i128`, so an extreme amount can trap instead of returning a token error.|Replace the additions with `checked_add` and map failure to a new appended `TokenError` variant;Add tests that minting or transferring near `i128::MAX` returns the error instead of panicking;Keep existing discriminants unchanged and update the module docs|The workspace release profile enables `overflow-checks`, so this can trap on-chain
medium|[medium] Add paginated views for services and leases|`contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`, `contracts/registry/src/storage.rs`|There is no way to enumerate registered services or leases on-chain, so every dashboard and indexer must replay the full event history from the first ledger.|Add bounded list views such as `get_services(start, limit)` and `get_leases(start, limit)`;Cap `limit` with a documented constant to keep work per call bounded;Return an explicit end marker or short page so callers can page deterministically|Ids are monotonic from `ServiceCount` and `LeaseCount`
medium|[medium] Index services by provider|`contracts/registry/src/storage.rs`, `contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`|A provider with many services has no on-chain way to list its own services, and consumers cannot discover everything one operator offers.|Add a per-provider index with documented TTL semantics;Update it in `register_service` and keep it correct under a future service transfer;Add a paginated owner-scoped read view with tests|`DataKey` is append-only for compatibility
medium|[medium] Bound rate_per_call with a documented maximum|`contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`, `contracts/registry/src/errors.rs`|Any positive `i128` rate is accepted, so the only upper bound is an overflow deep inside deposit math, which is hard for clients to predict.|Add a `MAX_RATE_PER_CALL` constant justified in the PR;Reject rates above it with a new appended error or a documented existing error;Test the accepted and rejected boundaries and document the value|`create_lease` already uses `checked_mul`
medium|[medium] Bound the maximum deposit a single lease can escrow|`contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`, `contracts/registry/src/errors.rs`|A lease can escrow an arbitrarily large deposit as long as the consumer balance supports it, which makes consumer mistakes and token-scale changes unnecessarily expensive.|Add a `MAX_LEASE_DEPOSIT` constant and reject deposits above it before calling the token;Append an error variant rather than reusing `Overflow`;Test exactly at the limit and one unit above, and document the bound|The check belongs after the checked rate multiplication
medium|[medium] Add a read-only quote_lease view|`contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`|Consumers and frontends compute the deposit, expiry and required balance off-chain and can drift from contract semantics.|Add `quote_lease(service_id, requested_calls, duration_secs)` returning deposit and expiry;Reject inactive or unknown services with the same errors as `create_lease`;Cover the view with tests for valid, paused and overflowing inputs|Reuse the validation order from `create_lease`
medium|[medium] Track cumulative settlement stats per service|`contracts/registry/src/storage.rs`, `contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`|Providers and indexers must recompute lifetime payouts and settled call counts from the event stream to report revenue.|Store settled call and payout totals keyed by service id;Update them in `settle_lease` before the token transfers and extend their TTL;Expose a read view and test that revoked leases do not contribute|Per-service state needs a bounded TTL policy
medium|[medium] Allow an authorized delegate to create a lease for a consumer|`contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`|A consumer that keeps its funds in a cold account cannot let a hot service create leases without handing over the spending key.|Add a delegate or sponsor path that authenticates both the delegate and the consumer, or an allowance-style approval;Define exactly whose balance is debited and who owns the refund;Test that a delegate cannot create a lease without the consumer's authorization|Stellar smart-wallet accounts already support multisig auth
medium|[medium] Represent revocation distinctly from settlement|`contracts/registry/src/types.rs`, `contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`|`LeaseStatus` reports `Settled` for both provider settlement and consumer revocation, so clients must inspect events to tell a paid lease from a refunded one.|Add a distinct `revoked` field or a `LeaseStatus::Revoked` variant using only additive ABI changes;Update `LeaseStatus::of`, the views, docs and tests;Decide how a revoked lease reads through `used_calls` and document it|Discriminants in `LeaseStatus` are documented as stable
medium|[medium] Version the event schema|`contracts/registry/src/events.rs`, `docs/contract-reference.md`|Event payloads have no version marker, so an additive or changed field forces indexers to guess which contract revision emitted an event.|Add a documented schema version to the contract metadata or each event;Keep the change additive and assert it in the event tests;Document how an indexer should branch on the version|Event names and fields are part of the ABI
medium|[medium] Stop extending TTL from read-only views|`contracts/registry/src/storage.rs`, `contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`|`get_service` and `get_lease` call `extend_ttl`, so a supposedly read-only simulation performs a write and can charge rent or fail under a read-only auth context.|Separate pure reads from bump operations and keep TTL maintenance in state-changing paths or an explicit permissionless bump;Measure the fee difference with a test and record it in the PR;Update the storage docs to state which calls extend TTL|`get_service` currently extends on every call
medium|[medium] Add an explicit permissionless bump_lease entry point|`contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`, `contracts/registry/src/storage.rs`|Once reads stop extending TTL, a long-lived lease needs a way to keep its entry alive without settling it.|Add `bump_lease(lease_id)` that extends the entry using the existing lease TTL policy;Decide whether it is permissionless and document the abuse bound;Test that it cannot shorten a TTL and that a missing lease returns `LeaseNotFound`|`lease_ttl_extend` is already the policy implementation
medium|[medium] Clamp lease TTL extension to the network maximum|`contracts/registry/src/storage.rs`|`lease_ttl_extend` derives an extension from the lease term, and the result is only clamped to `u32::MAX` rather than to a documented maximum entry TTL.|Introduce a named maximum TTL constant below the network limit and clamp to it;Test the clamp at the maximum lease duration and at a timestamp far past expiry;Document the relationship between the lease bound and the TTL bound|`MAX_LEASE_DURATION_SECS` is thirty days today
medium|[medium] Exercise the full lifecycle with a custom SEP-41 token|`contracts/registry/src/test.rs`|The suite covers the mock token and the native SAC but never a deployed custom SEP-41 contract through the registry, which is a supported configuration.|Deploy a custom token in the test and run register, create, settle and revoke through the registry;Assert balances on the custom token at every step;Include a partial settlement after expiry|`test_escrow_runs_on_the_stellar_asset_contract` is the closest existing test
medium|[medium] Test a token that rejects transfers|`contracts/registry/src/test.rs`|A misbehaving or paused SEP-41 token can fail a transfer, and the registry must leave no half-written lease or settlement behind.|Install a token whose transfer always returns an error;Assert `create_lease` fails and no lease is readable and no counter moved;Assert a failed settlement keeps the lease unsettled and the escrow intact|Soroban rolls back host state when an invocation fails
medium|[medium] Test a re-entrant token during revoke|`contracts/registry/src/test.rs`|Only settlement has a re-entrancy test, while `revoke_expired_lease` also marks state and then transfers value.|Install a token that calls back into `revoke_expired_lease` during the refund;Assert the second call fails with `LeaseAlreadySettled`;Assert the consumer is refunded exactly once|Model it on `test_reentrant_token_cannot_settle_a_lease_twice`
medium|[medium] Test a re-entrant token during create_lease|`contracts/registry/src/test.rs`|`create_lease` saves the lease before the deposit transfer, and a token callback could try to create or settle a lease while the first call is still in flight.|Have the token call back into `create_lease` and assert the re-entrant call is rejected or safely independent;Assert the outer lease and all balances are correct;Record the expected behaviour in the test name and docs|The deposit transfer is the cross-contract boundary
medium|[medium] Add a 100-lease stress test|`contracts/registry/src/test.rs`|Per-lease bugs and counter drift can hide in small tests, and a larger batch exercises storage isolation and TTL extension at scale.|Create one hundred leases across several services and consumers;Settle and revoke a mix of them and assert conservation of value;Assert counters and every lease id remain consistent|Keep the test deterministic and fast enough for CI
medium|[medium] Benchmark instruction budgets for each entry point|`contracts/registry/src/test.rs`, `.github/workflows/ci.yml`|Soroban charges for CPU instructions, and a refactor can increase them without any test noticing until users pay more.|Measure instructions for register, create, settle and revoke with the test budget API;Record a documented ceiling per entry point with headroom;Fail CI when a ceiling is exceeded and print the measured value|Soroban test environments expose CPU instruction accounting
medium|[medium] Report test coverage and enforce a floor|`.github/workflows/ci.yml`, `Cargo.toml`|Coverage is unknown and can regress quietly as new entry points and branches are added.|Run `cargo llvm-cov` in CI and publish the summary;Set a coverage floor that the current suite passes, not an aspirational number;Exclude generated or test-only code explicitly rather than lowering the floor|`contracts/mock_token` currently has no tests
medium|[medium] Add cargo-deny license and advisory checks|`.github/workflows/ci.yml`, `deny.toml`|The advisory scan is tracked separately, but licenses and duplicate dependency versions are not checked at all.|Add a `deny.toml` with an explicit allowed-license list and the project's policy for duplicates;Run `cargo deny check` in CI as its own named job;Document how to add an exception with a justification|`cargo-audit` is already proposed in another issue
medium|[medium] Snapshot the public contract ABI|`contracts/registry/src/test.rs`, `contracts/registry/src/errors.rs`|Nothing prevents an accidental rename or removal of an entry point, event or error code, even though all three are public interfaces.|Create a checked-in ABI snapshot covering exported functions, event names and error discriminants;Add a test that compares the current contract surface to the snapshot;Document how a legitimate additive change updates the snapshot|Error codes are already documented as append-only
medium|[medium] Verify reproducible Wasm builds in CI|`.github/workflows/ci.yml`, `docs/deployments.md`|The deployment docs publish a Wasm hash, but CI never proves that the same source produces the same bytes.|Build the release Wasm twice from clean targets and compare hashes;Fail with both hashes when they differ;Print the hash so it can be compared with `docs/deployments.md`|`docs/deployments.md` records the current hash
medium|[medium] Publish a CycloneDX SBOM with CI artifacts|`.github/workflows/ci.yml`|Consumers have no machine-readable inventory of the crates compiled into the deployed Wasm.|Generate a CycloneDX SBOM from `Cargo.lock` in CI and upload it with the workflow artifact;Include the workspace version and commit in the SBOM metadata;Link the artifact from the release notes template|`Cargo.lock` is tracked in the repository
medium|[medium] Upload built Wasm and its hash from CI|`.github/workflows/ci.yml`|Reviewers cannot download the exact artifact a PR produces, and the hash is only visible by reading logs.|Upload both release Wasm files and a `sha256sum` file as a workflow artifact;Name the artifact with the commit SHA for traceability;Keep the existing build job's time budget in mind|`keylease_registry.wasm` and `keylease_mock_token.wasm` are produced by the workspace build
medium|[medium] Build a docs preview artifact for pull requests|`.github/workflows/docs.yml`|Documentation changes are only validated on main, so a broken nav or anchor is discovered after merge.|Add a pull_request workflow that runs `mkdocs build --strict` and uploads the site artifact;Do not deploy the preview;Cache the pip install the way the deploy workflow does|`docs.yml` already builds with `--strict`
medium|[medium] Scan pull requests for committed secrets|`.github/workflows/ci.yml`, `.gitleaks.toml`|A contributor can accidentally commit a Stellar secret seed or RPC credential, and no check looks for it.|Add gitleaks or an equivalent scanner on pull_request with a pinned version;Add an allowlist only for documented false positives;Fail the job on findings and document local usage|`SECURITY.md` covers disclosure, not prevention
medium|[medium] Add a verify-deployment script|`scripts/`, `docs/deployments.md`|After a deploy, verification is a copy-pasted sequence of `stellar contract invoke` calls, which is easy to run against the wrong contract id.|Add a script that reads the contract id from `.env` and checks `version`, `get_admin`, `get_token` and demo service;Print a clear pass or fail per check and exit non-zero on a mismatch;Document it in the deployment guide|`docs/deployments.md` has a Verifying a listed address section
medium|[medium] Add a post-deploy smoke test|`scripts/`, `.github/workflows/ci.yml`, `docs/deployment.md`|Nothing proves a fresh deployment can actually escrow and settle a lease on the chosen network until a human runs the flow.|Add an opt-in script that creates a one-call lease and settles it with zero or one verified call;Guard it behind explicit environment variables so it cannot run against production by accident;Print the transactions and clean up by letting the lease expire if needed|`scripts/deploy-testnet.sh` already prints every id needed
medium|[medium] Add a --dry-run mode to deploy-testnet.sh|`scripts/deploy-testnet.sh`|Operators cannot see the resolved admin, provider, token, rate and endpoint hash without sending transactions.|Add `--dry-run` that validates inputs, builds nothing or only prints the plan, and prints every command with resolved values;Make it refuse to touch the network;Document the flag in the header and `docs/deployment.md`|The script already resolves defaults before deploying
medium|[medium] Guard deploy-testnet.sh against accidental mainnet use|`scripts/deploy-testnet.sh`, `docs/deployment.md`|`NETWORK` is user-controlled, so a typo or an exported variable can point the script at mainnet before the contract is audited.|Refuse `NETWORK=mainnet` unless an explicit opt-in variable is set;Print a prominent warning and the resolved network before any send;Add a test or dry-run check for the guard|Mainnet is intentionally deferred in `docs/deployments.md`
medium|[medium] Add bats tests for the shell scripts|`tests/`, `.github/workflows/ci.yml`|Argument parsing, missing variables and id validation in the scripts are untested, and failures appear only during a real deployment.|Add bats tests for create-issues, setup-repo and deploy-testnet argument handling;Stub `gh` and `stellar` on PATH so no network or credentials are needed;Run the suite in CI|Keep the tests in a new `tests/` directory
medium|[medium] Add an upgrade wrapper script|`scripts/`, `docs/deployment.md`|Upgrading requires three manual commands and the docs warn that storage must be preserved, but nothing enforces the sequence.|Add a script that builds, uploads, invokes `upgrade`, then calls `version` and compares it with the workspace version;Require an explicit contract id and admin identity;Print the Wasm hash and the upgrade event hint|`deploy-testnet.sh --upgrade` is the current manual path
medium|[medium] Add a local-network sandbox script|`scripts/`, `docs/developer-guide.md`|Local end-to-end testing requires manually starting a network, funding identities and deploying both contracts.|Add a script that starts or connects to a local Stellar network, deploys the registry and a token, and writes an `.env.sandbox`;Make teardown explicit and never touch Testnet or Mainnet;Document the workflow in the developer guide|`stellar network` and `stellar contract` provide the primitives
medium|[medium] Run clippy against the Wasm target|`.github/workflows/ci.yml`|Clippy currently runs for the host target, so target-specific warnings and `no_std` issues in the deployed artifact can be missed.|Add `cargo clippy --target wasm32v1-none --release --all -- -D warnings` to CI;Fix or explicitly justify any findings;Keep the job name stable for branch protection|The Wasm build job already installs the target
medium|[medium] Forbid unsafe code and std leakage in contract crates|`contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`, `contracts/mock_token/src/lib.rs`|`#![no_std]` is present, but nothing forbids `unsafe` and a dependency or test-only path could still pull in `std`.|Add `#![forbid(unsafe_code)]` to both contract crates;Add a CI check that the release Wasm builds without std and fails if `std` is linked;Document the policy in `CONTRIBUTING.md`|The contracts already target `wasm32v1-none`
medium|[medium] Label pull requests by the paths they touch|`.github/labeler.yml`, `.github/workflows/labeler.yml`|Maintainers triage every PR by hand even though the repository layout maps cleanly to contracts, docs, CI and scripts.|Add an actions/labeler configuration for `contracts/registry`, `contracts/mock_token`, `docs`, `scripts` and `.github`;Pin the action version and grant only the permissions it needs;Verify a sample PR gets the expected labels|Labels already exist for Wave tiers
medium|[medium] Add a tagged release workflow|`.github/workflows/release.yml`, `CHANGELOG.md`|Releases are cut by hand, so the version, changelog, Wasm artifact and tag can drift apart.|Add a tag-triggered workflow that builds the Wasm, records hashes, extracts the matching release-notes file and creates a GitHub release;Fail if the tag does not match the workspace version;Attach the Wasm and hash file to the release|`docs/releases/` already holds per-version notes
medium|[medium] Warn on missing public documentation|`contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`, `contracts/mock_token/src/lib.rs`, `.github/workflows/ci.yml`|Public entry points and types are documented by convention, but new public items can land undocumented without failing any check.|Add `#![warn(missing_docs)]` to the contract crates and `-D warnings` in the docs build;Document any intentional exceptions;Add a short note to `CONTRIBUTING.md`|The codebase already uses module and item docs
medium|[medium] Property-test the lease TTL calculation|`contracts/registry/src/storage.rs`, `contracts/registry/src/test.rs`|`lease_ttl_extend` combines remaining time, grace and a read buffer, and its arithmetic is only covered indirectly by lifecycle tests.|Add a property test for the TTL function over arbitrary expiry and ledger timestamps;Assert the result is monotonic in remaining time and never exceeds the documented maximum;Assert it always covers expiry plus the grace window|The function uses saturating arithmetic
medium|[medium] Extract a shared registry test harness|`contracts/registry/src/test.rs`|Each test rebuilds the same environment, token, admin, provider and service setup, which makes new tests verbose and inconsistent.|Add a small helper or macro that returns a ready registry with funded accounts and one service;Migrate a few tests first and show the reduction;Keep every test's ledger time explicit rather than hiding it|The file already has local constants and setup snippets
medium|[medium] Upload lint and audit results as SARIF|`.github/workflows/ci.yml`|Findings from clippy and the advisory scan are only visible in logs, not in the repository's code-scanning view.|Emit SARIF from clippy and the advisory scanner and upload it with the code-scanning action;Grant only the required security-events permission;Document where maintainers see the results|GitHub code scanning accepts SARIF uploads
medium|[medium] Document the dependency-update policy|`CONTRIBUTING.md`, `.github/dependabot.yml`|There is no stated rule for how quickly a soroban-sdk or transitive advisory update is applied or who reviews it.|Write the policy covering security updates, minor updates and major soroban-sdk upgrades;Tie it to the advisory-scan job and the review requirements;Add a checklist for an upgrade PR that includes the Wasm hash check|`soroban-sdk` is workspace-pinned
medium|[medium] Test that an upgrade preserves storage|`contracts/registry/src/test.rs`, `contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`|The upgrade path claims to keep storage and in-flight leases, but the suite only checks that a non-admin cannot upgrade.|Deploy the registry, create a service and lease, then install a Wasm and assert the same storage is readable;Assert `get_admin`, `get_token` and counters survive;Document which Wasm fixture the test uses|`update_current_contract_wasm` is the upgrade primitive
medium|[medium] Reject an all-zero endpoint_hash|`contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`, `contracts/registry/src/errors.rs`|`endpoint_hash` is the only commitment to the off-chain service descriptor, and an all-zero value is accepted as if it were a real commitment.|Reject a zero 32-byte hash on `register_service` with an appended error;Add a test for zero and one-byte-nonzero values;Document that the hash must commit to the descriptor, not be a placeholder|The type is `BytesN<32>` in `types.rs`
medium|[medium] Make set_token a no-op when the token is unchanged|`contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`|Calling `set_token` with the current token rewrites instance storage and emits an event that suggests a configuration change.|Return early or document why the repeat write is intentional;Test that the no-op path does not emit a misleading event;Keep the first-time and changed-token paths covered|`set_token` already emits `escrow_token_set`
medium|[medium] Include rate_per_call in the lease_created event|`contracts/registry/src/events.rs`|Indexers need the service's rate to interpret `locked_deposit`, but the event only carries the deposit and they must read the service at the event's ledger.|Add `rate_per_call` as an additive field on `lease_created`;Update the event tests, contract reference and integration table;Document that the value is a snapshot at creation time|`LeaseCreated` already carries `locked_deposit`
medium|[medium] Record created_at on each lease|`contracts/registry/src/types.rs`, `contracts/registry/src/storage.rs`|A lease does not store when it was created, so consumers and indexers cannot compute age or reconstruct terms without the event history.|Add a `created_at` ledger timestamp to `Lease` using an append-only storage change;Decide the upgrade implications for existing stored leases and document them;Assert the value in create and read tests|`expires_at` is stored but creation time is not
medium|[medium] Add a runnable event-indexer example|`examples/indexer/`, `docs/integration.md`|The docs describe an indexer in prose, but there is no runnable reference that filters topics and reconciles settlements.|Add a minimal example that follows `lease_created` and `lease_settled` from Soroban RPC or the event stream;Persist a lease ledger keyed by id and print payouts and refunds;Document how to run it against Testnet with a read-only identity|`docs/integration.md` has the verification pipeline
medium|[medium] Add a consumer command-line helper|`examples/consumer/`, `docs/consumer-guide.md`|Consumers copy long `stellar contract invoke` commands and can easily pass the wrong service id or forget the grace window when revoking.|Add a small script that reads `.env`, validates the service, creates a lease and prints the lease id and expiry;Add status and revoke subcommands with the same validation;Keep all signing in the Stellar CLI keystore|`docs/consumer-guide.md` documents the manual flow
